<h2>What are CSS variables?</h2>
<hr />
<p>
  CSS variables (more formerly known as custom properties) are standardised values such as specific
  colours and numbers which can be reused throughout a project. We use CSS variables all over the
  website in several areas, ensuring that pk-gov's design is as consistent as possible. The CSS
  variables used in pk-gov can be found in the 'variables.css' file (located in styles/ with the
  other .css files).
</p>

<h2>Typography variables</h2>
<hr />
<h3>font-family</h3>
<p>
  There are two font-family variables available, which should be chosen according to the
  <a class='link' href='/dev/design/gov-identity/'>Government identity guidelines</a>
  . Note that code blocks such as the ones shown below use the user's default monospace font, no
  specific font is chosen!
</p>
<ul>
  <li style='font-family: var(--plex-sans)'>
    <code>--plex-sans:</code>
    sets the font-family of the styled element to IBM Plex Sans, with the default sans-serif font as
    a fallback.
  </li>
  <li>
    <code>--mona-sans:</code>
    sets the font-family of the styled element to Mona Sans, with the default serif font as a
    fallback.
  </li>
</ul>
<h3>font-weight</h3>
<p>
  There are two font-weight variables available, which should be chosen according to the
  <a class='link' href='/dev/design/gov-identity/'>Government identity guidelines</a>
  . The fonts used on pk-gov are variable fonts, so they can theoretically be set to a wide range of
  font-weights beyond the standard ones here if needed.
</p>
<ul>
  <li style='font-weight: var(--font-weight-bold)'>
    <code>--font-weight-bold</code>
    sets the font-weight of the styled element to 700. This is applied to all heading elements
    (h1-6) by default.
  </li>
  <li>
    <code>--font-weight-normal</code>
    sets the font-weight of the styled element to 400. This is applied to all elements by default.
  </li>
</ul>
<h3>line-height</h3>
<p>
  There are two line-height variables available. Always choose the higher line-height option
  available for a better reading experience, unless it makes content look less appealing.
</p>
<ul>
  <li style='line-height: var(--line-height-relaxed)'>
    <code>-line-height-relaxed</code>
    sets the line-height of the styled element to 1.5. This should always be the first choice, as
    larger line-heights generally make it easier for users to read content than lower line-heights.
  </li>
  <li style='line-height: var(--line-height-condensed)'>
    <code>--line-height-condensed</code>
    sets the line-height of the styled element to 1.2. This should only be used if the relaxed
    line-height makes content look "awkward" or waste too much space, such as for larger headings.
  </li>
</ul>
<h3>font-size</h3>
<p>
  There are eight common font-size variables available. Font sizes are usually automatically set by
  CSS, but can be changed if needed. There is also one "outlier" variable
  <code>--font-size-pibss-value</code>
  which should NOT be used in normal situations, as it sets the font-size to an excessive 10rem,
  only used for the key statistics values in PIBSS.
</p>
<ul>
  <li style='font-size: var(--font-size-xxs)'>
    <code>--font-size-xxs</code>
    sets the font-size of the styled element to 0.75rem.
  </li>
  <li style='font-size: var(--font-size-xs)'>
    <code>--font-size-xs</code>
    sets the font-size of the styled element to 1rem.
  </li>
  <li style='font-size: var(--font-size-s)'>
    <code>--font-size-s</code>
    sets the font-size of the styled element to 1.5rem.
  </li>
  <li style='font-size: var(--font-size-m)'>
    <code>--font-size-m</code>
    sets the font-size of the styled element to 2rem.
  </li>
  <li style='font-size: var(--font-size-l)'>
    <code>--font-size-l</code>
    sets the font-size of the styled element to 2.5rem.
  </li>
  <li style='font-size: var(--font-size-xl)'>
    <code>--font-size-xl</code>
    sets the font-size of the styled element to 3rem.
  </li>
  <li style='font-size: var(--font-size-xxl)'>
    <code>--font-size-xxl</code>
    sets the font-size of the styled element to 4rem.
  </li>
  <li style='font-size: var(--font-size-xxxl)'>
    <code>--font-size-xxxl</code>
    sets the font-size of the styled element to 5rem.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Spacing (padding and margin) variables</h2>
<hr />
<h3>Primitive values</h3>
<p>
  There are 11 common primitive spacing variables available (8 positive, 3 negative), used to adjust
  padding/margin of elements that are not targeted by the semantic values. There is one "outlier"
  variable
  <code>--content-max-width</code>
  which should NOT be used in normal situations, as it is only used to ensure content remains at a
  reasonable width for ultrawide displays. Note that negative spacing variables ONLY work with
  margin.
</p>
<ul>
  <li style='padding-left: var(--spacing-xxs); box-shadow: inset var(--spacing-xxs) 0 0 0 orange'>
    <code>--spacing-xxs</code>
    gives 8px of spacing. (
    <code>--spacing-negative-xxs</code>
    provides the same amount of negative spacing)
  </li>
  <li style='padding-left: var(--spacing-xs); box-shadow: inset var(--spacing-xs) 0 0 0 orange'>
    <code>--spacing-xs</code>
    gives 16px of spacing. (
    <code>--spacing-negative-xs</code>
    provides the same amount of negative spacing)
  </li>
  <li style='padding-left: var(--spacing-s); box-shadow: inset var(--spacing-s) 0 0 0 orange'>
    <code>--spacing-xxs</code>
    gives 24px of spacing.
  </li>
  <li style='padding-left: var(--spacing-m); box-shadow: inset var(--spacing-m) 0 0 0 orange'>
    <code>--spacing-m</code>
    gives 32px of spacing. (
    <code>--spacing-negative-m</code>
    provides the same amount of negative spacing)
  </li>
  <li style='padding-left: var(--spacing-l); box-shadow: inset var(--spacing-l) 0 0 0 orange'>
    <code>--spacing-l</code>
    gives 40px of spacing.
  </li>
  <li style='padding-left: var(--spacing-xl); box-shadow: inset var(--spacing-xl) 0 0 0 orange'>
    <code>--spacing-xl</code>
    gives 48px of spacing.
  </li>
  <li style='padding-left: var(--spacing-xxl); box-shadow: inset var(--spacing-xxl) 0 0 0 orange'>
    <code>--spacing-xxl</code>
    gives 64px of spacing.
  </li>
  <li style='padding-left: var(--spacing-xxxl); box-shadow: inset var(--spacing-xxxl) 0 0 0 orange'>
    <code>--spacing-xxxl</code>
    gives 80px of spacing.
  </li>
</ul>
<h3>Semantic values</h3>
<p>
  There are 13 semantic spacing variables available, each used for specific contexts. Some are used
  for "normal" elements appearing on desktop-sized viewports, while others are for "compact"
  elements appearing on mobile-sized viewports.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <code>--content-width-limit</code>
    is used to center content on ultrawide displays. This is automatically applied where it is
    needed.
  </li>
  <li
    style='padding-top: var(--header-search-buffer); background-image: linear-gradient(orange, orange); background-size: 100% 10rem; background-repeat: no-repeat;'
  >
    <code>--header-search-buffer</code>
    is equivalent to 10rem and ensures the heading is visible when users search for it. This is
    automatically applied for searchable headings.
  </li>
  <li style='border: var(--print-margin) solid orange'>
    <code>--print-margin</code>
    is equivalent to 18mm, used only for the margin in printed web pages.
  </li>
  <li>
    <code>--spacing-negative-logo</code>
    is used to adjust the spacing between 'Plushie Kingdom' and 'Government' in the header, and
    should NOT be used in normal situations.
  </li>
  <li style='border: solid orange; border-width: var(--spacing-header)'>
    <code>--spacing-header</code>
    is used to provide the spacing for the header.
  </li>
  <li style='border: solid orange; border-width: var(--spacing-input-normal)'>
    <code>--spacing-input-normal</code>
    is used to provide the spacing for normal input elements.
  </li>
  <li style='border: solid orange; border-width: var(--spacing-input-compact)'>
    <code>--spacing-input-compact</code>
    is used to provide the spacing for compact input elements.
  </li>
  <li style='border: solid orange; border-width: var(--spacing-section-normal)'>
    <code>--spacing-section-normal</code>
    is used to provide the spacing for normal content sections.
  </li>
  <li style='border: solid orange; border-width: var(--spacing-section-compact)'>
    <code>--spacing-section-compact</code>
    is used to provide the spacing for compact content sections.
  </li>
  <li style='border: solid orange; border-width: var(--spacing-card-normal)'>
    <code>--spacing-card-normal</code>
    is used to provide the spacing for normal cards.
  </li>
  <li style='border: solid orange; border-width: var(--spacing-card-compact)'>
    <code>--spacing-card-compact</code>
    is used to provide the spacing for compact cards.
  </li>
  <li style='border: solid orange; border-width: var(--spacing-footer-normal)'>
    <code>--spacing-footer-normal</code>
    is used to provide the spacing for the normal footer.
  </li>
  <li style='border: solid orange; border-width: var(--spacing-footer-compact)'>
    <code>--spacing-footer-compact</code>
    is used to provide the spacing for the compact footer.
  </li>
</ul>

